  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 155 PM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nFor the rest of the day, the widespread rainfall from early \nthis morning has largely moved through the area. However, \nscattered showers are still expected to linger into this \nafternoon for far northeast Minnesota and northwest Wisconsin, \nwhich will squeeze out a few more hundredths of an inch of \nprecipitation. As the rain showers depart, patchy fog is \nexpected to linger in their wake. This will be particularly \nnoticeable around the Brainerd Lakes area into the evening. \nCloud cover will remain in place tonight with lows dropping \ninto the upper 30s and lower 40s across the Northland.\n\nOur active weather pattern continues right into Wednesday as another\nlow pressure system and mid-level wave approach from the Dakotas.\nThis will spread another round of widespread showers across the\nregion. Instability that will accompany these showers tomorrow will\nlead to embedded thunderstorms during the Wednesday afternoon and\nevening hours. Though the overall chances for severe weather remain\nlow, some communities could see small, pea-sized hail for a brief\nperiod sometime in the late afternoon. South to southwest winds\nwill also be quite breezy ahead of this system, bringing warm\nair into the region from the south and temperatures will be \ninto the upper 50s and mid 60s for most locations.\n\nThe rain comes to an end late Wednesday night as a cold front sweeps\nthrough, leading to a quiet end to the work week. High pressure\nwill build in for Thursday and Friday, bringing a return to \nplentiful sunshine across the region. With northwest winds \nexpected behind the departing system, temperatures will be a \nbit cooler on Thursday, with highs topping out in the upper 40s \nto mid 50s. Pleasant conditions will hold through Friday, with \nhigh temperatures moderating back into the mid 50s to lower 60s \nunder mostly clear to partly cloudy skies.\n\nHeading into the weekend, the pattern becomes active once \nagain. Global models point to a deepening upper-level trough \nmoving into the central United States, bringing broad chances \nfor precipitation to the Northland from Saturday through Monday.\nWhile the exact timing and placement of these weekend showers \nremain a bit uncertain, multiple rounds of rain are possible. \nDespite the clouds and rain, temperatures will remain \nwarmer than normal through the weekend. Highs will consistently\nreach the mid 50s to lower 60s, while overnight lows will \ncomfortably stay above freezing, generally resting in the upper \n30s and 40s.",
